{
  // Anti-dead-code gate. Knip finds what ESLint's `no-unused-vars` can't:
  // whole unused files, exports/types no other module imports, and
  // dependencies declared in package.json but never used (plus the inverse —
  // imports with no matching dependency). Run with `npm run knip`; enforced by
  // .github/workflows/knip.yml.
  //
  // Every issue type knip reports is an error — that is its default severity,
  // so this file sets no `rules`. The pre-existing backlog is held at its
  // current size instead: `npm run knip` runs knip twice, once over the
  // classes that must be clean, then once over the parked ones under
  // `--max-issues`. `classMembers`, `nsExports` and `nsTypes` sit in knip's
  // own `defaultExcludedIssueTypes`, so the second pass names them explicitly
  // — without that they are gated by neither pass and a new unused class
  // member costs nothing. Naming `classMembers` also turns knip's `isSkipLibs`
  // off (see `isSkipLibs` in its create-options), which suppresses 44 of the
  // export/type findings the same run reports without it: 60/187 becomes
  // 47/156. That, not the 12 classMembers findings it adds, is why the ceiling
  // is lower than the two counts alone would suggest — `--include-libs` with no
  // classMembers at all reproduces 47/156 exactly.
  //
  // The ceiling is one total across those classes, so it bounds the backlog
  // rather than each class: paying off one parked export does licence one new
  // one elsewhere in the same PR. It drops as follow-up passes pay it down, and
  // a change that legitimately adds an export nothing imports yet raises it in
  // the same commit — that bump is the thing review should see. Two such PRs
  // make the same one-line edit and merge without conflicting, leaving the
  // number one short of the truth; the push-triggered run on main is what
  // catches that.
  //
  // Count it the way CI does, in a tree with no build output. With
  // `isSkipLibs` off the whole 215-vs-208 difference hangs on a single path:
  // whether packages/registry's `main`/`types` resolves to a file that exists.
  // Not on anything in that file — a zero-byte packages/registry/dist/index.js
  // takes the count to 208 just as a real build does — and not on any other
  // workspace's, twelve of which move it by nothing at all. So `npm run knip`
  // passing after a local `tsc --build` is not evidence the job will pass; it
  // has seven slots of headroom CI does not. Remove the dist directories
  // before trusting a number from this ceiling.
  //
  // Never reported as unused: build output (git-ignored, so knip skips it as a
  // file, even where it moves the counts above),
  // node_modules (excluded unconditionally), the argent-private submodule
  // (excluded below), and every file that is not JavaScript or TypeScript
  // (knip's DEFAULT_EXTENSIONS) — though workflows and package manifests are
  // still read as plugin input.
  "$schema": "https://unpkg.com/knip@5/schema.json",

  // Stale exemptions in this file are a failure, not a footnote: this makes
  // knip's configuration hints exit non-zero like any other finding — an
  // `ignoreDependencies` name that suppresses nothing, an `entry` pattern that
  // matches nothing or is already reached, an `ignoreWorkspaces` path that does
  // not exist. The gap is an `ignoreWorkspaces` directory that exists without a
  // package.json, which is the uninitialized submodule below: it suppresses
  // nothing and is not hinted. Should that directory go missing altogether the
  // hint does fire, advising you to drop the entry — which is the one thing not
  // to do. Restore the directory instead. (`git clean -ffdx` will not do it:
  // clean skips a path the index holds as a gitlink.)
  "treatConfigHintsAsErrors": true,

  // The submodule ships from its own repo and has its own CI. It becomes a
  // workspace only once `git submodule update --init` puts a package.json
  // under packages/argent-private for the root `packages/*` glob to match —
  // this entry is what keeps it out of the gate then. eslint and prettier skip
  // it the same way.
  "ignoreWorkspaces": ["packages/argent-private"],

  "workspaces": {
    // Root workspace: build/dev/release/CI scripts. Knip reaches these on its
    // own, from package.json's `scripts`, from the scripts/*.test.mjs files
    // those run, and from `run:` steps in .github/workflows — its
    // github-actions plugin reads multi-line `run: |` blocks too. What the
    // plugin does not parse is a PowerShell body, so the Windows E2E driver —
    // invoked from the windows-latest job, where that is the default shell —
    // is declared here by hand.
    //
    // Scope limit: only 12 of the 31 files under scripts/ are in the gate at
    // all, because knip analyzes JS/TS and nothing else. The other 19 — 15
    // `.sh` files, e2e-full/lib/report.py, ci/e2e-chromium-page.html, and
    // e2e-full's README and results/.gitignore — are invisible to it however
    // they are reached, which covers the whole scripts/e2e-full/ harness —
    // invoked from nothing outside its own directory, the only mention of it
    // elsewhere being a .prettierignore entry.
    ".": {
      "entry": ["scripts/ci/windows-chromium-e2e.mjs"],
    },

    // @swmansion/argent is a thin dispatcher whose real payload is an esbuild
    // bundle of the OTHER workspaces (see scripts/bundle-tools.cjs). The
    // hand-written dispatcher copied into bin/ at build time is an entry, not
    // dead code.
    //
    // Knip cannot follow imports across that esbuild boundary, so every
    // package consumed only by the bundled-in code reads as unused here. Those
    // are named one by one rather than waved through with a catch-all pattern:
    // `ignoreDependencies` also suppresses `unlisted`, so a catch-all would
    // take this workspace's own src/ and scripts/ out of that check — where an
    // import with no matching declaration is a real bug, not a bundler
    // artifact. (It reaches `unresolved` only nominally: knip matches this list
    // against a package name parsed out of the specifier, and an unresolvable
    // relative path yields none, so even a catch-all leaves those reported.
    // `ignoreUnresolved` is the option that does silence them.)
    //
    // Half-checked: knip folds `optionalDependencies` into the declared set, so
    // importing `electron` or `node-pty` here is not `unlisted` — they pass
    // because it read them. What it never reports is an optionalDependency
    // nothing uses, so that direction is outside the gate, as is argent-cli's
    // runtime `req("node-pty")`, resolved through a
    // `createRequire(import.meta.url)` in lens-pty.ts.
    "packages/argent": {
      "entry": ["scripts/argent-simulator-server.cjs"],
      "ignoreDependencies": [
        // dependencies
        "@fails-components/webtransport",
        // Not bundled-in code reaching it: @fails-components/webtransport
        // imports this itself at runtime, so nothing first-party imports it.
        "@fails-components/webtransport-transport-http3-quiche",
        "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk",
        "tree-sitter",
        "tree-sitter-typescript",
        // devDependencies
        "@clack/prompts",
        "picocolors",
        "posthog-node",
        "semver",
        "smol-toml",
        "yaml",
      ],
    },

    // tool-server reaches a few packages in ways knip can't trace statically.
    // `ignoreDependencies` works in both directions, so this one list silences
    // two different issue classes; they are kept apart below so each entry's
    // stated reason matches the finding it actually suppresses.
    //
    // Each entry exempts the NAME across the whole workspace, not the call
    // site it documents: while `sharp` is listed, an undeclared import of it
    // from any file here passes too. Knip offers nothing narrower, so the list
    // holds only packages this workspace genuinely cannot declare.
    "packages/tool-server": {
      "ignoreDependencies": [
        // --- Declared in package.json, loaded too dynamically to detect.
        //     Without these: "unused dependencies". ---
        // Dynamic `_require("tree-sitter")` / `_require("tree-sitter-typescript")`
        // in react-profiler/pipeline/.../ast-index.ts, behind a one-shot flag.
        "tree-sitter",
        "tree-sitter-typescript",
        // Optional peers of @swmansion/argent-cloud-sdk, which leaves them to
        // the host to declare. utils/moq-client.ts calls the SDK's
        // `installNodeWebTransport()`, but the dynamic import of the polyfill
        // sits inside the SDK's own /node entry, so no first-party file names
        // it; the transport is a second hop, imported by the polyfill itself.
        "@fails-components/webtransport",
        "@fails-components/webtransport-transport-http3-quiche",

        // --- Imported here but deliberately absent from this package.json.
        //     Without these: "unlisted dependencies". ---
        // Lazy `require("sharp")` in chromium-server/screenshot.ts. Kept out of
        // the manifest on purpose so the ~30 MB native binary isn't a cost every
        // consumer pays; the caller degrades gracefully when it isn't installed.
        "sharp",
        // `require("electron")` twice, for two different reasons. In
        // utils/preview-window.ts it resolves the Electron executable's path
        // from the install root, where it is an optionalDependency of the
        // published @swmansion/argent. In test/fixtures/electron-smoke-app it
        // is the Electron main-process API; that fixture declares electron in
        // its own package.json and CI installs it there, but knip attributes
        // the file to this workspace's manifest and never reads the nested one.
        "electron",
      ],
    },
  },

  // Blind spot in the `files` check: test files are entry points, so a
  // production module that nothing but its own test imports still counts as
  // reached. Being reached by a test says nothing about being reachable in
  // production, which is what this check is meant to approximate — and two
  // modules sit in exactly that gap. src/tools/paste/index.ts is imported by
  // two test files and registered by nothing, so it is unreachable over MCP
  // and HTTP; src/utils/ios-profiler/lifecycle.ts is a re-export barrel whose
  // header keeps it "for source compatibility with existing imports", of which
  // its own test is the only one — all four production consumers import
  // profiler-shared/lifecycle directly.
  //
  // Those two are the current occupants, not the extent of it: any module that
  // ships with a test is invisible to `files` the same way, which in this repo
  // is most of them. What `files` reliably catches is a module with no test at
  // all. A file orphaned inside test/ is invisible too: root package.json's
  // `test:scripts` runs `node --test`, which is what makes knip's node plugin
  // contribute its test entries, and those are four patterns in every
  // workspace — `**/test/**`
  // plus `*{.,-,_}test`, `test-*` and `test` matched anywhere — each limited to
  // {cjs,mjs,js,cts,mts,ts}. So all 420 JS/TS files under packages/*/test/ are
  // entries, and under packages/*/tests/ every name matching one of the three
  // is too: of today's 16 that leaves registry/tests/helpers.ts as the only
  // reportable one, which is why it is the lone `tests/` entry in the
  // --production list below. A `tests/test-utils.ts` or `tests/test.ts` would
  // be just as invisible. The extension set is the escape hatch: it omits .tsx
  // and .jsx though DEFAULT_EXTENSIONS covers them, so an orphaned .tsx under
  // test/ IS reported. The repo has no .tsx or .jsx at all today, which is why
  // that whole test/ tree is out of reach rather than most of it.
  //
  // Reachability has a wider escape hatch still: whatever a workspace's entry
  // re-exports is reached. One `export { x } from "./dead-module";` line in a
  // src/index.ts takes that module out of `files` altogether, and re-exporting
  // parked exports retires them from the count without deleting anything —
  // three such lines in tool-server's entry take 215 to 212. So the ceiling
  // bounds what knip can see, not what is dead.
  //
  // `knip --production` is the lens that reports the whole class, and the repo
  // is not clean under it: 13 unused files — those two, seven root scripts,
  // packages/argent/scripts/{bundle-tools,observe,sync-readme}.cjs and
  // packages/registry/tests/helpers.ts — with the export/type backlog at
  // 243/212. That run has classMembers off, so those two compare to the 60/187
  // above, not to the ceiling's 47/156. Paying it down is a follow-up of its
  // own.
}
